{
  "reconstructed_theorem_text": "For every finite-dimensional complete real inner-product space E equipped with its compatible norm and Borel measurable structure, and every real-valued function f on E that is globally twice continuously Frechet differentiable and has compact support, let gamma be the canonical standard Gaussian probability measure on E. Equivalently, if (e_i) is its canonical orthonormal basis and the Z_i are independent centered real Gaussian variables of variance one, gamma is the law of the sum of Z_i e_i. In dimension zero this is the point mass at the zero vector. Let grad f(x) be the real Hilbert-space Riesz representative of the Frechet derivative Df(x), so Df(x)[v] = <grad f(x), v> for every v in E.\nThen f squared, f squared times log(f squared), and the squared Hilbert norm of grad f are integrable with respect to gamma. Writing m = integral_E f(x)^2 dgamma(x), one has\nintegral_E f(x)^2 log(f(x)^2) dgamma(x) - m log(m) <= 2 integral_E ||grad f(x)||^2 dgamma(x).\nAll integrals are real Bochner integrals over E. The logarithm is the total real logarithm with log(0) = 0, so the expression u log(u) has value zero when u = 0. The function f may take either sign, need not have unit squared mass, and may have zero squared mass. Integrability is established in the conclusion rather than supplied as an assumption. The constant 2 is universal, independent of the dimension, space, and function. This statement is limited to the stated finite-dimensional setting and globally C2 compactly supported functions; it asserts no extension to other laws, arbitrary covariance, infinite-dimensional spaces, or weaker regularity classes.",
