  "domains": "f and its global derivatives have domain all of E. Values of f are arbitrary real numbers. gamma is a probability measure on the Borel sets of E, obtained from independent centered variance-one real Gaussian coordinates under the canonical orthonormal basis sum, including the empty sum and Dirac law in rank zero. grad f(x) lies in E. Norms are the compatible Hilbert norms; no coordinate sup-norm energy is substituted. Every displayed integral is a real Bochner integral over the whole E with respect to gamma. The entropy uses nonnegative f^2, and the total real logarithm, including log(0) = 0.",
  "quantifiers": "Universally quantified over E, the specified compatible algebraic/topological/measurable structures, and every f : E -> real. For each such f, globally C2 Frechet smoothness and compact support imply all three integrability conclusions and the entropy bound. gamma is fixed by E inside the conclusion; it is not an independently supplied measure. There are no extra existential witnesses, covariance parameters, supplied transport maps, normalization conditions, or premise certificates for the desired inequality.",
  "assumptions": "E is a normed additive commutative group and real inner-product space, complete and finite-dimensional over the reals, with a measurable space satisfying BorelSpace. f is ContDiff real 2 on all E and HasCompactSupport. These are the analytic premises. No sign restriction, strict positivity, nonzero mass, unit mass, prior L1/Sobolev-domain certificate, measure-law identification certificate, Parseval certificate, or inequality certificate is assumed.",
  "conclusion": "Conjunction: (1) f^2 is gamma-integrable; (2) f^2 log(f^2) is gamma-integrable; (3) ||grad f||^2 is gamma-integrable; (4) Ent_gamma(f^2) = integral_E f^2 log(f^2) dgamma - (integral_E f^2 dgamma) log(integral_E f^2 dgamma) <= 2 integral_E ||grad f||^2 dgamma. Entropy is the homogeneous square entropy, without a denominator or unit-mass restriction.",
  "scopes": "C2 regularity and compact support are global conditions on f. The gradient is the global Frechet derivative represented through the real inner product, not an independently supplied field or a weak derivative choice. The law is exactly the canonical standard Gaussian of E. Integrability and the bound are simultaneous consequences for this same function and actual law. Rank zero, signed f, and zero squared mass are admitted. The conclusion is bounded to this function class and finite-dimensional geometry; no general Sobolev closure, arbitrary Gaussian covariance, other distribution, or infinite-dimensional assertion is made.",
